WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.239
The squire went to Bristol to outfit a ship and get us ready to go to sea, and it took longer than he thought.

2
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:28.316
The doctor had to go to London to seek a physician to take over his practice, and I stayed at the squire's house with Tom Redruth, his loyal gamekeeper.

3
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:37.702
I spent all of my time brooding over the map, and I was full of sea dreams and anticipations of strange islands.

4
00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:44.679
But I never imagined anything as strange and tragic as what our actual adventures would be.

5
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:51.079
After I'd spent many weeks daydreaming by the fire, Tom came to me and said, "This letter arrived.

6
00:00:51.080 --> 00:01:01.779
It's addressed to Dr. Livesey but says ‘To be opened, in his absence, by Tom Redruth or young Hawkins.' I can only read print, so you read it."

7
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.916
I opened the letter and read aloud:

8
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:09.985
Old Anchor Inn, Bristol, March 1, 1746

9
00:01:09.986 --> 00:01:11.861
Dear Livesey—

10
00:01:11.862 --> 00:01:17.271
I don't know whether you are at home or in London, so I'm sending this letter to both places.

11
00:01:17.272 --> 00:01:23.269
The ship is ready, and you won't believe how sweet she is—a child could sail her!

12
00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:30.266
Her name is Hispaniola, and my old friend Blandly purchased her for a trifle and worked hard to get her ready for us.

13
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:38.180
So did everyone in Bristol, as soon as they got wind of what we'd be searching for—treasure, I mean.

14
00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:41.611
"Oh, Dr. Livesey won't like that," I commented.

15
00:01:41.612 --> 00:01:44.604
"He told the squire not to talk about the treasure."

16
00:01:44.605 --> 00:01:49.581
"He can talk if he wants—he's the squire!" Tom grumbled.

17
00:01:49.582 --> 00:01:54.335
I gave up any more attempts at commentary and continued to read.

18
00:01:54.336 --> 00:01:56.955
Finding a crew was difficult.

19
00:01:56.956 --> 00:02:05.895
I needed about twenty men, and I had a devil of a time finding even half a dozen, but then the most remarkable luck brought me the very man I required.

20
00:02:05.896 --> 00:02:10.811
I was standing on the dock, and by accident, I started talking with him.

21
00:02:10.812 --> 00:02:16.517
He's an old sailor who owns a tavern and knows all the seafaring men in Bristol.

22
00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:22.113
He told me he'd been ashore a long time, and wanted a position as cook to get to sea again.

23
00:02:22.114 --> 00:02:26.733
He'd hobbled down there that morning, he said, just to breathe the salt air.

24
00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:31.951
I was touched and, out of pure pity, I hired him on the spot to be the ship's cook.

25
00:02:31.952 --> 00:02:40.747
He's called Long John Silver and he lost a leg in the service of England—which I thought was a strong recommendation as to his character.

26
00:02:40.748 --> 00:02:45.952
He helped me find the crew, and we put together a company of the toughest old sailors imaginable—

27
00:02:45.953 --> 00:02:50.448
not pretty to look at, but they have the most indomitable spirit.

28
00:02:50.449 --> 00:02:59.690
Long John even got rid of two of the men I'd already hired, after he showed me they wouldn't be suitable for an adventure of this importance.

29
00:02:59.691 --> 00:03:06.989
I'm eating like a bull and sleeping like a tree, yet I shall not enjoy a moment until we are under way. Seaward, ho!

30
00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:13.331
Let young Hawkins stop to see his mother, with Tom Redruth, and then both should come to Bristol immediately.

31
00:03:13.332 --> 00:03:15.886
—John Trelawney

32
00:03:15.887 --> 00:03:25.071
Postscript—Blandly also found an admirable fellow to captain the ship; he's a bit businesslike and stiff, but in all other respects, a treasure.

33
00:03:25.072 --> 00:03:34.288
And Long John Silver unearthed a very competent first mate, a man named Arrow, so things shall go very well on board the good ship Hispaniola.

34
00:03:34.289 --> 00:03:36.482
J. T.

35
00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:45.474
"Hooray!" I shouted, gleeful to be starting our adventure, even if I did have to travel with a grumpy old man like Tom Redruth.

36
00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:52.281
The next morning, we set out on foot for the Admiral Benbow, and I found Mother in good spirits.

37
00:03:52.282 --> 00:03:58.966
"The squire arranged for repairs, some repainting, and new furniture," said Mother.

38
00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:02.449
"There's even a beautiful new armchair for me.

39
00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:08.053
He also hired a boy, so I won't need help while you're gone," she added.

40
00:04:08.054 --> 00:04:12.739
All I had thought of up until then had been the adventures in front of me,

41
00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:19.843
but meeting this boy who would be taking my place made me feel homesick already and a bit angry.

42
00:04:19.844 --> 00:04:25.125
"You missed a spot," I said to him, pointing out a dirty area on the table he was cleaning.

43
00:04:25.126 --> 00:04:33.510
The next day, after dinner, I said good-bye to Mother, and the cove where I'd lived since I was born, and the dear, old Admiral Benbow.

44
00:04:33.511 --> 00:04:40.656
Within minutes, Tom Redruth and I turned a corner in the road and my home was out of sight.

45
00:04:40.657 --> 00:04:45.459
A stagecoach picked us up at dusk, and I dozed the whole way.

46
00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.251
When I opened my eyes, it was morning and we were in a bustling city.

47
00:04:50.252 --> 00:04:52.614
"Where are we?" I asked.

48
00:04:52.615 --> 00:04:56.331
"Bristol," Tom said. "Get out."

49
00:04:56.332 --> 00:05:03.063
Mr. Trelawney was rooming at an inn down by the docks to supervise the work being done on the ship.

50
00:05:03.064 --> 00:05:11.587
Tom and I started walking to the inn, and on the way, we saw a great multitude of ships of all sizes and from all nations.

51
00:05:11.588 --> 00:05:21.027
On one ship, sailors were singing at their work, and on another, men were aloft, high up on ropes that seemed no thicker than a spider's thread.

52
00:05:21.028 --> 00:05:30.116
"I can't believe I'm going to sea, to an unknown island, on a ship with singing seamen!" I said excitedly to Tom.

53
00:05:30.117 --> 00:05:36.173
Suddenly, we came to the inn and met Squire Trelawney, who was all dressed up like an admiral.

54
00:05:36.174 --> 00:05:43.869
"Here you are," he cried. "The doctor came last night. Bravo! The ship's company is complete!"

55
00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.532
"Oh, sir," I cried, "when do we sail?"

56
00:05:47.533 --> 00:05:57.061
"Sail?" he said with a laugh. "We sail tomorrow!"
